WEBVTT

00:00:03.108 --> 00:00:06.998
The second conversation is about people: who are we?

00:00:07.048 --> 00:00:09.118
Teams are not org charts.

00:00:09.168 --> 00:00:14.238
They are people with different strengths, ways of working, and things going on in their lives.

00:00:14.288 --> 00:00:18.258
When teammates don’t know each other, they fill the gaps with assumptions.

00:00:18.308 --> 00:00:24.098
And assumptions are where most friction comes from: “She never answers quickly, she must not care.”

00:00:24.148 --> 00:00:28.378
When actually, she needs an hour of quiet before she replies to anything.

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:33.058
Signs your team needs this conversation: new people have joined recently.

00:00:33.108 --> 00:00:36.798
People work side by side but rarely ask each other for help.

00:00:36.848 --> 00:00:41.098
Misunderstandings keep happening about how people prefer to communicate.

00:00:41.148 --> 00:00:46.418
Or the team only knows each other’s job titles, not what each person is great at.

00:00:46.468 --> 00:00:55.098
The output on the canvas is three things: the strengths the team has, what it wants to develop, and how each person works at their best.

00:00:55.148 --> 00:00:59.478
There are three exercises for this conversation, and you can pick the one that fits.

00:00:59.528 --> 00:01:06.738
“The story behind me” is a warm-up for connection: everyone brings a photo or object and tells the story behind it.

00:01:06.788 --> 00:01:10.598
“Personal user manual” makes working preferences explicit.

00:01:10.648 --> 00:01:16.078
And “Skills marketplace” shows what people can offer each other and where they want to grow.

00:01:16.128 --> 00:01:19.398
For most teams, I’d start with the personal user manual.

00:01:19.448 --> 00:01:22.518
It gives the most practical value in the shortest time.

00:01:22.568 --> 00:01:29.232
Use the story exercise when the team is new, or when trust is low and people need to see each other as humans first.

